WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.400
Follow power from the shelf into rack distribution and then into the

2
00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:03.840
equipment.

3
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.640
A power shelf converts its input and supplies the distribution path.

4
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.380
The busbar carries current to the equipment connection.

5
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.260
Power-entry circuitry controls how the equipment accepts that input.

6
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.640
Every part has an operating envelope.

7
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.340
The forty-eight volt architecture name doesn't mean every cited design uses exactly

8
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.100
forty-eight volts as its nominal output.

9
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.900
The base specification includes defined voltage options; the high-power shelf example we'll

10
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.220
use later specifies a fifty volt output.

11
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.420
Match the equipment's accepted range to the actual source and revision rather

12
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.280
than substituting the architecture label for a voltage requirement.

13
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.260
As the path builds in the animation, notice that the available capacity

14
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.420
is constrained by more than the supply units.

15
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.520
Connections, busbars, thermal conditions and the equipment entry can impose tighter limits.

16
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.580
A larger source can't make an underspecified downstream connection adequate.

17
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.740
Record power at a defined boundary, together with the stated operating and

18
00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:57.520
fault conditions.

19
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.960
We'll use that same discipline when calculating usable capacity.

